module System

# Each declaration below creates nothing: `@rv` names the shared declaration it shows, and validate-stdlib
# checks that the two still match.

### A routine value whose signature is known only at run time: the routine value and a hash of its signature (its
### parameter and result types, each with its module). Values of different signatures fit in one place
### (`List[ErasedCallable]`). `f.to[ErasedCallable]()` makes one, and `e.to[Callable[(Int,), Int]]()` gives the routine
### value back when the signatures match. What a lambda captured is let go when the last copy goes, as for a routine
### value.
@rv("ErasedCallable")
record ErasedCallable
    nothing

### Makes an `ErasedCallable` of a routine value. The routine value stays usable.
### :returns: The routine value with its signature hash.
@rv("T.to")
routine T.to[ErasedCallable]() -> ErasedCallable
needs RoutineType T

### Gives the routine value back as the routine type `T` it was made from.
### :returns: The routine value.
### :throws: {RoutineSignatureError} if the routine value has another signature than `T`.
@rv("ErasedCallable.to")
routine ErasedCallable.to[T]() -> T
needs RoutineType T

### Whether the routine value has the signature of the routine type `T`.
### :returns: True when `to[T]()` would give it back.
@rv("ErasedCallable.fits")
routine ErasedCallable.fits[T]() -> Bool
needs RoutineType T

### An `ErasedCallable` was asked for a routine type its routine value doesn't have.
@rv("RoutineSignatureError")
crashable RoutineSignatureError
    ### The routine type that was asked for.
    asked: Text

### Returns the message naming the routine type that was asked for.
### :returns: The message.
@rv("RoutineSignatureError.crash_message")
routine RoutineSignatureError.crash_message() -> Text
